# Data Layer & Supabase Patterns

## Client Selection Matrix
| Context | Client | Import |
|---------|--------|--------|
| Server Component / API Route | Server client | `lib/supabase/server` |
| Client Component / Hook | Browser client | `lib/supabase/client` |
| Migration / Admin task | Admin client | `lib/supabase/admin` |

Never use the admin client in user-facing API routes — it bypasses RLS.

## Query Design
```typescript
// ❌ BAD — select * wastes bandwidth
const { data } = await supabase.from('profiles').select('*');

// ✅ GOOD — explicit column selection
const { data } = await supabase
  .from('profiles')
  .select('id, username, avatar_url, bio')
  .eq('username', username)
  .single();
```

## Error Handling Pattern
```typescript
const { data, error } = await supabase.from('profiles').select('id').single();
if (error) {
  console.error('[data/profiles] fetch failed', { error });
  throw new DatabaseError(error.message, { cause: error });
}
```

## Migration Conventions
- One migration = one logical change. File: `NNN_<description>.sql` (e.g., `003_add_skills_table.sql`).
- Always define RLS policies in the same migration as the table.
- Add indexes on foreign keys and frequently filtered columns.
- Never mutate existing migration files — create a new one.

## RLS Rule
Every table must have RLS enabled with explicit policies. Default: deny all.
